WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.767
System-design interview drills.

2
00:00:03.067 --> 00:00:07.306
Four prompts cover about ninety percent of AI system-design interviews.

3
00:00:07.306 --> 00:00:09.933
Let's build the answers from the blueprints.

4
00:00:11.133 --> 00:00:13.033
Every answer starts with questions.

5
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:14.400
Ask about scale.

6
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.433
Ask about the latency target.

7
00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:17.933
Ask about freshness.

8
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:19.582
And ask about tenancy.

9
00:00:19.582 --> 00:00:22.033
The clarifying questions are the signal.

10
00:00:23.233 --> 00:00:25.933
Drill one: RAG over ten million documents.

11
00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:28.300
Parsing workers are fed by a queue.

12
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.033
Embed in batches, and quote the cost math.

13
00:00:31.333 --> 00:00:37.600
Use a sharded index, hybrid search and a reranker, because ID lookups are guaranteed at this size.

14
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.233
Keep it fresh with change detection feeding an upsert queue.

15
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:44.267
Drill two: a research agent.

16
00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:45.900
Plan with a task list.

17
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.100
Act with search, fetch and a note-taking scratchpad.

18
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.865
Reflect before the final output, all under budget caps.

19
00:00:52.865 --> 00:00:55.133
Human approval only at publish time.

20
00:00:56.333 --> 00:01:00.400
Drill three: serve an open model at a thousand requests per second.

21
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.596
Do the arithmetic out loud.

22
00:01:02.596 --> 00:01:05.967
Naively, that is on the order of a hundred GPUs.

23
00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:10.400
Then cut it with AWQ quantization and continuous batching.

24
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:14.200
Route about sixty percent of easy traffic to a smaller model.

25
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.064
And cache the head of the distribution.

26
00:01:17.064 --> 00:01:19.300
Autoscale on queue depth, not CPU.

27
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.833
Drill four: ship a prompt change safely.

28
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:25.233
Run the golden-set suite in CI.

29
00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:29.400
Use a pairwise judge, swapping the order to kill position bias.

30
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:34.033
Canary at five percent, with auto-rollback on a hallucination regression.

31
00:01:34.333 --> 00:01:37.067
Then roll out, with the prompt versioned and traced.

32
00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:38.844
The traps.

33
00:01:38.844 --> 00:01:44.100
Naming a framework first, or saying you would eyeball a few outputs, can end the interview.

34
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.400
Instead, lead with architecture and finish with the eval story and the cost per query.

35
00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:51.447
Let's recap.

36
00:01:51.447 --> 00:01:53.633
Ask clarifying questions first.

37
00:01:53.933 --> 00:01:55.733
Build the answer from the blueprints.

38
00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:57.633
Do the arithmetic out loud.

39
00:01:57.933 --> 00:02:00.933
And always end with the eval story and the cost story.
